Hi Farhad,

For your questions:
1. Why normalization is to be applied after summarization?
There are mainly two reasons. First, the large percentage of missing peptide identifications across LC-MS runs in TMT experiment makes it difficult to do normalization on peptides before summarization. Second, based on our evaluation, normalization after summarization shows a better testing performance than  normalization before summarization.

2. What is the difference between summarisation method “msstats” and “ MedianPolish”? In method msstats the summary method is still TMP!

You are right that “msstats” and “ MedianPolish” both use TMP as summarization methods. But “msstats”  has one additional step which imputes the missing values within each LC-MS run.

3. The vignette of the package is not explaining the statistical concepts behind TMT analysis. Would you please address me to some resources on this regard?

We are preparing the manuscript about MSstatsTMT package. We will update the citation information in MSstatsTMT webpage as it comes out.

4. In PDtoMSstatsTMTFormat, the the parameter “summaryforMultipleRows” is set to sum by default. Shouldn’t it be applied after normalisation?

The parameter “summaryforMultipleRows”  is used to deal with the case that one peptide matches with multiple spectra. MSstatsTMT selects the intensity from the best PSM as the final peptide intensity. Then MSstatsTMT summarizes the peptide intensities into protein intensity.

5. Normalization between MS runs will be done by using Norm channels. But don't we also need to normalise between channels after combining the fractions (or before that)?

The aim of normalization is to remove the technical variability in TMT data. And the advantage of TMT experiment is that all the samples in one MS run suffer the same technical biases. So we only do normalization between runs.

Best,
Ting

